model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "Every fleet lane failure in configure and disable says where it happened in closed values only. Each read and send carries one closed step name (probe, inspect, primary_read, send, inspect_after, digest) beside the error's class, and a refused handoff answer prints its HTTP status as a number, so an answer the bridge made reads apart from one the Worker made. Every runtime-handoff answer that leaves the pool's PoolOperations handoff code carries the pool's own mark, and the refusal prints handoff.response.origin as pool or unmarked. Unmarked means no answer left that code: a proxy on the bridge, a throw or platform failure in the pool's invocation before it, or a pool still on code from before the mark."
      constraint "configure-and-accept may run right after a deploy, so every read before its send, the code probe and the target-active pre-read included, shares one wait for the controller's code handover (330 s: the platform's 300 s reset onto a new Worker version plus transport), at most one read per 10 s poll, with nothing sent. A read lost on the way, a controller still on its old code refusing the new signature as slot_controller_code (slot_identity from code before that reason), and bindings that still lag the Worker settings wait. A typed refusal and any other Worker answer (its 409 refusal form or a reply the lane can't decode) stand at once. Past the window a lost read refuses as controller-not-ready, old code as controller-code-mismatch, and lagging bindings as controller-bindings-differ, so no release order has to know the timing. No wait begins once a bound has passed, so the last read can start up to one poll after it and the wait can end up to one poll and one read after it. inspect and retire-check send nothing, so their code probe waits the same way, but only for what is left of the window since the PoolWorker's latest deployment, because the lane holds the release lock: past it, or when that time can't be read, the probe reads once and refuses at once. A probe of theirs that did not settle at its first read logs one probe_wait line with the window left (or unread), its whole seconds, its reads and each wait as old_code or read_lost; the run's evidence carries the same fields, through any later failure too. Every other mode probes once and refuses old code at once."
      constraint "configure-and-accept disables the fleet on a failure only once its run sent a configuration, holds one under its GO, or disabled the fleet itself to drain old rows; a failure before that leaves a fleet another GO enabled as it was, and says nothing-sent. The acceptance tries the deploy smoke's models in order, moving on when one is refused for quota. When every one is refused for funding (usage_credit_policy_denied, Home's decision from the pool's accounts) it disables nothing: the configuration stays in force, unproven, and the run fails. A model_cooldown refusal, which a cell's own node can cause, disables like any cell that fails to serve."
      constraint "A configure refuses to relabel an occupied row whose application, version, image digest or input it does not name, so configure-and-accept reads the pins first (waiting out an application rollout for up to 330 s, before anything is sent) and, when rows run what the pins don't name, disables the fleet and waits until every row left is one the pins name. The wait is bounded by what ends a drain with no live stream: a still ledger count lowered after 300 s, a retirement within 105 s, and 30 s of upkeep passes (435 s). A live stream holds its cell past that; the run then refuses as stale-rows-remain naming each row's phase and stream count, with the fleet disabled and the root serving."
      constraint "A production deploy whose plan changes the pool Worker's POOL_BLUE_IMAGE_HASH, POOL_GREEN_IMAGE_HASH or either POOL_*_APPLICATION_ID leaves the fleet's permission naming other cells, so every launch is refused until it is re-committed. The plan prints that it does, naming the rows; the approved plan digest covers them and is the authority. After the apply and a passing smoke, the deploy runs configure-and-accept under an operation key of its own and requires the fleet in force with a cell serving; a fleet that is disabled or was never configured stays so, with nothing sent, and so does one disabled by anyone but its own drain while it runs: each send follows a read. A deploy with no such rows reads the permission and fails when an earlier run left it out of force. A failure there ends the deploy red without rolling it back, and its last line says whether scale-out is off and which lane run turns it back on; a deploy that dies inside that step gets the same line from one read in the step after it. A deploy that applied and passed its smoke is recorded as a successful deployment whatever its fleet step says."
      constraint "The Worker's handoff entry writes one closed POOL_RUNTIME_COMMAND_REFUSED line, with its status, for every answer but a 200: a 409 refusal, a 500 defect and the early 405 and 413 alike. Writing the line never changes the answer."
      constraint "A PoolOperations call into the controller that throws, before the controller runs (an argument RPC can't send) or mid-call (a reset or a lost connection), writes one closed POOL_OPERATIONS_CALL_FAILED line with the route, the status and the error's closed shape, as POOL_CONTROLLER_CALL_FAILED does, and answers that status, 500: the controller's own refusal line is never reached, and the lane prints the same status."
      constraint "A health read its own caller deliberately aborted, as the guard's probe does at its deadline, writes no POOL_RUNTIME_ENDPOINT_FAILURE: the guard keeps its own outcome and elapsed time. It is decided on the abort's reason, so the outcome never depends on which promise settles first: a timeout's reason always logs, and so does a send that failed before the abort, and every other aborted call."
      constraint "POOL_FLEET_GROWTH_REFUSED carries a provider refusal's closed code, read and reason beside its class, as the pending-pass lines and the recovery record do, through one shared helper, at info for an active rollout and warn for any other reason. Each slot whose start fails has its own episode on the controller's clock, opened by any failed try of that start (growth's first, a retry of its unsent start, or an observation of a start that doesn't answer yet), so a replaced isolate opens a fresh one at its next failed try. It logs POOL_FLEET_GROWTH_STILL_REFUSED once, at warn whatever the reason, at its first failed try after 30 s, and POOL_FLEET_GROWTH_RECOVERED once with its length and failed tries when that slot's admission has committed; both name the latest try's class, step and cause beside any provider reason; another cell's admission never ends it, and a slot that leaves unadmitted (given up, quarantined or gone) ends it without a line."
      constraint "Recovery keeps how the primary it restarts had ended as that boot's own end: each round records it before admitting a restart and only when none is kept for that boot, so a restart attempt that ends before it serves never replaces it, and the record changes only when a later boot ends in its turn."
      constraint "The controller keeps one record of the selected root's last recovery in closed values: the boot it restarts, when it was detected, when the root served again (after a restart, or on a healthy pass with none), its rounds, and the latest eight rounds' refusals, each pass counting against only its own round. A served root's next recovery starts a new record. It is written in its own storage transaction without the selection snapshot; healthy passes read it once per isolate and write only to end an open recovery, its writes never change what a round does, and the controller inspect returns it for the lane to print keyed by the restarted boot's short hash."
    }
  }
}
